{
  "result": "At 5:17, the city is not yet awake, but it is practicing. Delivery trucks sigh at curbs, their doors rolling upward like reluctant curtains. A cyclist crosses an empty intersection, red light blinking patiently above no one. In apartment windows, blue screens glow beside sleeping plants.\n\nI like this hour because it belongs to no single purpose. Bakers knead dough, nurses trade shifts, janitors unlock classrooms, and insomniacs make promises they may not remember by breakfast. Even the pigeons seem less certain of themselves, stepping carefully along wet pavement as if the world has recently been painted.\n\nBy eight o'clock, certainty returns. Phones ring. Elevators fill. People move with the bright, defensive speed of those who have been assigned somewhere to be. The city becomes useful again, and usefulness is impressive, but it is not always beautiful.\n\nBefore that transformation, there is a small freedom in being unnecessary. A fountain splashes for no audience. A newspaper tumbles down a sidewalk, carrying yesterday's arguments toward a storm drain. Somewhere, someone opens a window and lets cold air rearrange a room.\n\nPerhaps this is why dawn feels generous. It offers a brief interval in which nothing has completely failed, nothing has completely succeeded, and every ordinary object has another chance to become mysterious. The streetlamp, still lit against the paling sky, does not know it is outdated. It simply shines.\n\nFor a moment, its quiet persistence seems like advice: begin softly, remain present, and trust that morning will eventually find you again.",
